# examples/pipeline.vine handed something that is not its log. The refusal at
# the top of that program was written in tick 44 and nothing ran it until this
# case existed, which is how it kept a defect for a tick: the program collects
# a complaint per line it cannot read, and on the one run where those
# complaints are the *whole* story it used to end with a single sentence and
# drop every one of them. A log auditor going quiet exactly when the input is
# wrong is the failure a user meets first.
#
# So the three lines below are the three ways `read_event` can refuse a line,
# one each and in that order: an event word that is not start, ok or fail; a
# line with the wrong number of fields; and a timestamp with a minute of 61,
# which is five good fields and a well-formed-looking stamp that the clock
# rejects. All three are reported, and then the program refuses.
#
# The status is why this is a `.cli` case. In process the runner records a
# refusal as the case's own failure and cannot show the 1 the shell is handed
# -- see `tests/cases/cli/refusing.cli`, which makes the same point about
# `fail` itself.
examples/pipeline.vine
